alex: Quick sanity check please — PR #4827 updates how we wire per-model runtime knobs. Moving model-specific flags into a shared configmap in `redwood-models` ns and syncing via Terraform k8s provider. Planning to apply tonight after hours. Plan snippet below, can someone eyeball for churn/hot-reload risks?

alex: ```
+ resource "kubernetes_config_map" "permodel_flags" {
+   metadata {
+     name = "redwood-permodel-flags"
+     namespace = "redwood-models"
+     annotations = {
+       "redwood/config-checksum" = md5(file("${path.module}/permodel-values.yaml"))
+     }
+   }
+   data = { ... }
+ }
```

priya: Linking PR: https://git.internal/redwood/rf/4827 (config + TF + controller tweak).

sam: Quick question: does the TF change preserve the checksum annotation semantics we use for hot-reload? If TF rewrites ordering we might cause a full rollout.

alex: I added an MD5 checksum computed over a canonicalized YAML we generate (`permodel-values.yaml`) so TF should only change annotation when actual keys/values change. Rough snippet in the module: 

alex: ```hcl
locals {
  canonical = trimspace(chomp(external("./bin/canonicalize", ["${path.module}/raw-values.yaml"])))
}
resource "kubernetes_config_map" "permodel" {
  metadata {
    annotations = {
      "redwood/config-checksum" = md5(local.canonical)
    }
  }
}
```

leah: We saw a churn incident when tiny whitespace diffs changed the checksum and triggered restarts last month. Can we ensure canonicalize sorts keys and strips comments?

priya: +1. Compute checksum only from sorted keys/values. Also suggest a rollout strategy: canary 5% for 30m then 100% with a pause. Safer than full blast.

sam: Also important: how does the runtime sidecar watch CM events? If TF does create+delete (instead of update) the watcher sees delete->create and may reload pods twice.

alex: Current sidecar watches by label `permodel=true`. I can change it to watch by object name + checksum annotation to avoid reacting to irrelevant label flips, but that needs a tiny controller change. Do we want that pre-apply?

priya: If changing controller is low-risk, do it first. Otherwise we can apply in a staged fashion: 1) create new CM with `-v2` suffix, 2) update pods to read new name (canary subset), 3) switch rest. More steps but less surprise.

leah: For timing: staging canary first. We should also add a pre-apply plan-check job that runs `kubectl get cm redwood-permodel-flags -o json | jq '.metadata.uid'` and compares to post-apply UIDs so we detect replace vs update.

sam: I can review the controller PR today. Also reminder to run `kubectl get cm -o json | jq '.items[] | {name:.metadata.name, keys: (.data | keys)}'` after plan to diff keys.

alex: Thanks — I'll push two changes in the next 15m: (1) a small canonicalize script (yq-based) in TF pipeline, (2) controller tweak to watch name+annotation. Pushing now and will ping when PRs are ready for review.

alex: ```bash
# rough canonicalize step (called from TF external data)
yq eval -o=json 'sort_keys(.. | select(tag == "!!map"))' raw-values.yaml | jq -S -c . > permodel-values.yaml
md5sum permodel-values.yaml | cut -d' ' -f1
```

deploy-bot: CI: PR #4827 — unit tests passed, integration job queued.

priya: One more: confirm no secrets are being moved into the CM. We need KMS + secret sync notes if anything sensitive moves.

alex: No secrets — only flags/knobs. Sensitive creds still in Secret Manager and mounted via secrets controller.

leah: Sounds good. Plan: controller patch -> staging canary -> TF apply to prod tonight with 30m pause at 5% -> full rollout. @sam please review controller PR when you can. :thumbsup:

sam: Ack, will review within the hour.

alex: Thanks all — will update thread when PRs ready and when I kick the canary.
